metal2vulkan: blends into BGR10_XR attachments by the device's fixed function, on the codes (Scion)

Upstream:  metal2vulkan, https://github.com/steelbrain/metal2vulkan, at 720ef1ce9ae2a2068999a8675717418b9416283e, after
           AdrenoMetal's 16 patches to it and Scion's 0001 to 0003; tools/metal/mk-metal2vulkan.sh applies them in that order,
           then this.
Licence:   GPL-2.0-or-later OR LGPL-3.0-or-later, metal2vulkan's (its LICENSE); this patch is a change to it and under the same
           terms. Nothing of it is in Scion's MIT or APSL files.
Author:    the HakiOS authors (Scion), 14 November 2026.

Why. The Astronomy wallpaper (AegirPoster, NanoUniverse's aegir_* shaders) draws into a CAMetalLayer of BGR10_XR_sRGB and
blends there: SourceAlpha and OneMinusSourceAlpha for the earth, luna, sprites and stars, One and One for aegir_post_fsh.
metal2vulkan holds such an attachment as its raw word (R32_UINT), which no device blends, and computes Metal's blend in the
shader over the attachment's texel, read through rasterization-order input attachments or under the pixel interlock. PanVK v6
(the Galaxy S9's Mali-G72) offers neither, so all 29 of its blended pipelines were refused NotImplemented(Format) at Color(0)
and the wallpaper stayed black.

What. On a device that orders no framebuffer fetch (neither rasterizationOrderColorAttachmentAccess nor the pixel interlock)
and renders and blends A2R10G10B10_UNORM_PACK32, a BGR10_XR or BGR10_XR_sRGB color attachment renders through that view of
its word (metal::format::unorm_codes_view). The view's fields are the word's own (red in bits 20-29, green 10-19, blue 0-9,
Metal's two unused bits as alpha), so every pipeline and pass on such a device renders the attachment through it, blended or
not, and the pass and its pipelines agree on its format:

- Unblended, the fragment function writes Metal's code over 1023 (and alpha 1, the two top bits Metal writes): exact.
- The codes are affine in Metal's value, u(v) = (384 + 510 v) / 1023, so the device's blend s u_s + d u_d of codes is the code
  of Metal's blend s v_s + d v_d where the function writes u_s = (510 v_s + 384 b) / 1023, b = (1 - d) / s, the share of the
  bias the source keeps (pipeline::lower_inputs::UnormWrite, pipeline::color_writes::unorm_write):
    Over          SourceAlpha / OneMinusSourceAlpha, a blend colour's or blend alpha's pair, One / Zero: b = 1;
    Added         any destination factor One with a source factor that reads no colour of the source or destination: b = 0
                  (a source below the code of 0 stores as 0, as the view clamps it);
    Premultiplied One / OneMinusSourceAlpha: b = the source alpha.
  These are Metal's blend up to the device's rounding of the blended code; the source is not rounded to a code first (its value
  is clamped as Metal's blend clamps it, codes 0 to 1022). The alpha channel is masked out under a blend (the top bits kept).
  Every other blend (a factor of the source or destination colour, the destination alpha, a second source, an operation other
  than add) stays refused NotImplemented(Format).
- An _sRGB attachment's codes hold the value encoded through Metal's sRGB tables, so a blend of codes is a blend of encoded
  values, not Metal's blend of linear ones: the function writes the code of Metal's encode of the source, and the device blends
  codes as a device without sRGB blending blends 8-bit sRGB bytes. An approximation, said here and in UnormWrite's docs: exact
  for an unblended write and at the factors' ends (an alpha of 0 or 1), darker than Metal's in between where the colours differ.
- The image of such a texture takes the view as a format it is viewed in (MUTABLE_FORMAT with a format list), memoryless ones
  too; the render pass renders the attachment in it (render_pass::attachment_format), and its clear value is the raw texel's
  fields over their largest (clear_value::in_format), which store the texel exactly.
- The cache's SEMANTICS_VERSION and ARTIFACT_FORMAT_VERSION move: ExtendedRangeColor has the field `unorm`.

Checked on the phone (PanVK on the Mali-G72): metal_tri --xr none, over and add (BGR10_XR, blended by BlendAlpha 0.25 and by
One / One, a texture and a texture over an IOSurface) store every pixel within 1 code of Metal's store of the expected value,
the clear's codes exactly outside; metal_libcheck --format 554 and 555 makes all 27 of NanoUniverse's functions unblended and
under --blend over, add and premultiplied, where 0 of the blended ones were made before.

